#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129034 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/id #uuid "fdf51620-58e9-5886-93e5-02a814b154bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8905" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=e^x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=0$ to $x_2={2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1+({e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129035 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129216 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/id #uuid "ae72af2a-ec7e-569d-89a8-550c8eeabdbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-8909" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ that lies between the lines $x=2$ and $x=4.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=2$ to $x_2=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x-2})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{\\frac{4x-7}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-7}}{2\\sqrt{x-2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=4x-7 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm du = 4\\,\\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129036 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129217 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "fcfc9339-efd3-525f-a6f3-695124724e1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9120" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^3-y$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?\n\n![](images/1b/1bdbe21cbb517645dfe9362f3313c2d85118acfea1d839fe33ae9b9ac6c3ed50.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}-y)=3{y}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{(3{y}^{2}-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{2-6{y}^{2}+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
